<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mouseion</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mouseion was a research institution founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> under the early Ptolemaic kings, dedicated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muses">Muses</a>. It housed scholars and scientists supported by the state and was closely linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria">Library of Alexandria</a>. It made Alexandria the leading intellectual centre of the Hellenistic world and is a key legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mouseion.jpg" alt="Mouseion" width="309" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous (Greece)Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greek_-_Leaning_Muse,_Probably_Polyhymnia_-_Walters_2390.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word mouseion originally meant any place dedicated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muses">Muses</a>, often connected with the study of music or poetry, and later it came to be associated with sites of learning such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s Academy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s Lyceum. According to Johannes Tzetzes, the Mouseion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I Soter</a>, though it more probably took shape under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-ii-philadelphus">Ptolemy II Philadelphus</a>. It arguably included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria">Library of Alexandria</a>. The Ptolemies reputedly set out to bring together some of the best scholars of the Hellenistic world and to collect all the books then known.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Mouseion was a community gathered under the protection of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muses">Muses</a>, supported by the patronage of the Ptolemies and later of the Roman emperors. It held no collection of sculpture and painting, unlike the one that their rival Attalus assembled at the Library of Pergamum. It was rather an institution of learning, compared by Germain Bazin to the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton. Reports are few, but scholars and staff appear to have been salaried by the state and to have paid no taxes; according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> they also received free room and board and free servants.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, in the first century CE, described it as part of the Brucheion, the palace complex, with a lobby, a columned hall and a large dining hall for the common table of its members, under a priest once appointed by the kings and now by Caesar. Scholars there evidently pursued literary criticism, and translated some foreign texts from Egyptian, Assyrian, Persian, Jewish and other languages. Many editions of the Greek canon, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a> onwards, rest on texts collated and corrected by scholars presumably linked to it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-viii-physcon">Ptolemy VIII Euergetes II</a>, in a time of political turmoil, most intellectuals were killed or expelled from the city, among them the last recorded head librarian, Aristarchus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samothrace">Samothrace</a>, who is said to have resigned in 145 BCE. The Mouseion continued under Roman rule but never regained its former standing, and its membership came to include politicians and athletes rewarded for service to the state. The Brucheion was probably destroyed by fire on the orders of Emperor Aurelian in 272 CE.</p><p>Its members included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callimachus">Callimachus</a>, who produced the first comprehensive book catalogue, the Pinakes, Zenodotos, the first head librarian, Apollonius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, author of the Argonautica, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eratosthenes">Eratosthenes of Cyrene</a>, who measured the Earth's circumference. Through their work the writings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> and the tragedians survive. The word mouseion is the root of the modern museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mouseion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was home to the Library of Alexandria and the <b>Mouseion</b>, which attracted scholars such as Euclid, Eratosthenes and Herophilus, and to the Pharos lighthouse, one of the Seven Wonders of the ancient world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
